<Window x:Class="D47.Panel.MainWindow"
        x:ClassModifier="internal"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:render="clr-namespace:D47.Render;assembly=D47.Render"
        Title="Directive 47"
        AutomationProperties.Name="Directive 47">

    <!--
        Deliberately no Height or Width. The controls decide how big the render
        is and the window opens around them, which is a number nobody can type
        here — it is worked out in MainWindow.xaml.cs, once, as the window is
        being created. What used to be here was 960x720, chosen before there was
        anything in the window to disagree with it.

        Deliberately no SizeToContent either, which is the WPF way to say the
        same thing and says too much of it. It fits the window to its content
        every time the content changes, so the size stops being the Commander's
        the moment a view is switched. It was tried anyway, on the theory that it
        could be turned off again once it had fitted the window once, and there
        is no event that fires between the two: the layout pass that applies the
        fit reports itself finished before the new size is on the window, so
        turning it off there froze the panel at the smallest size Windows will
        draw.

        Deliberately no Background. There is one render, it fills the window, and
        the background is a property of what that render draws — see
        D47.Render/Palette.cs. This window naming one of its own is how it came
        to disagree with the render in the first place.
    -->

    <!--
        Below what fits, it scrolls. The fitted size is where the window opens
        and the size at which everything is visible at once, not a floor the
        Commander is held to — so a window dragged smaller than its controls
        moves them rather than shrinking them.

        Vertically only, and that is the reflow rule rather than a shortcut.
        Content that runs out of width wraps and goes down the window; content
        that runs out of height has nowhere left to go, which is where scrolling
        starts. Horizontal scrolling would take the wrapping away — a child
        measured against infinite width never wraps at all — and put a second
        scrollbar in place of it.
    -->
    <DockPanel>

        <!--
            The zoom, shown and changeable. Ctrl and the wheel is what most
            people reach for and is also invisible: a Commander who has never
            used it cannot find out the panel zooms, and one who has zoomed by
            accident cannot see that they did.

            Docked rather than drawn over the render, so it covers nothing. The
            window fits the render plus this strip, which is the same rule #96
            already set — the window is as big as the controls in it, and this is
            one of them.

            Its colours come from the render's palette rather than from anywhere
            here, for the reason above: one source, so the strip cannot come to
            disagree with what it sits under. Its typeface deliberately does not
            — Saira is tuned for reading a render across a cockpit, and this is
            chrome, which is the one thing on the surface that should not compete
            with what it frames.
        -->
        <Border x:Name="ZoomStrip"
                DockPanel.Dock="Bottom"
                Background="{x:Static render:Palette.Background}"
                Padding="8,4">

            <DockPanel LastChildFill="False">

            <!--
                What to do about an update, on the surface that has a pointer.
                The notice itself is drawn by the render, so all three surfaces
                say the same thing; these two are panel chrome, like the zoom
                controls beside them, because Elite captures the cursor and the
                motion controllers belong to the game. Answering in the headset
                is voice, and arrives with the loop.

                Collapsed until there is something to answer, and collapsed
                again once it has been answered.
            -->
            <StackPanel x:Name="AboutTheUpdate"
                        Orientation="Horizontal"
                        DockPanel.Dock="Left"
                        VerticalAlignment="Center"
                        Visibility="Collapsed">

                <Button x:Name="TakeIt"
                        Content="Install on exit"
                        Click="AcceptTheUpdate"
                        Padding="10,1"
                        AutomationProperties.Name="Install on exit" />

                <Button x:Name="LeaveIt"
                        Content="Not now"
                        Click="DeclineTheUpdate"
                        Margin="6,0,0,0"
                        Padding="10,1"
                        AutomationProperties.Name="Not now" />

            </StackPanel>

            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal"
                        DockPanel.Dock="Right">

                <Button x:Name="Smaller"
                        Content="&#8722;"
                        Click="ZoomOut"
                        Width="28"
                        AutomationProperties.Name="Zoom out" />

                <TextBlock x:Name="Reading"
                           Width="56"
                           Margin="6,0"
                           TextAlignment="Center"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center"
                           Foreground="{x:Static render:Palette.BodyText}"
                           AutomationProperties.Name="Zoom" />

                <Button x:Name="Bigger"
                        Content="+"
                        Click="ZoomIn"
                        Width="28"
                        AutomationProperties.Name="Zoom in" />

            </StackPanel>

            </DockPanel>

        </Border>

        <!--
            An AutomationId so that what the panel is showing can be told from
            what the panel says about itself. The strip is furniture and the
            render is the answer, and a test asking whether the answer reached
            the screen has to be able to draw that line.

            The name beside it was left off on purpose — a name is read aloud and
            a scrolling region has nothing to say — and that was wrong. #167:
            leaving it off does not produce silence, it produces WPF's fallback,
            which walks down and stringifies whatever it finds. What it found
            here was the Answer record, so the region announced the whole object
            dump, type names and backing-field spellings included.

            So it is named for the least noisy true thing, and the name is not
            optional. A reader hears the region, then the answer inside it.
        -->
        <ScrollViewer AutomationProperties.AutomationId="TheRender"
                      AutomationProperties.Name="The render"
                      VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                      HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
            <render:CapabilityView x:Name="View" />
        </ScrollViewer>

    </DockPanel>

</Window>
